<BaseLayout title="Editorial Policy | DollarAngle" description="How DollarAngle separates facts from opinion, handles sources, investment coverage, corrections and conflicts.">
<section class="page-hero"><div class="container"><span class="eyebrow">Editorial standards</span><h1>Trust is part of the product.</h1><p>Our editorial process is designed to separate facts from opinion, explain uncertainty and keep financial education useful without turning it into personalized advice.</p></div></section>
<section class="section" style="padding-top:10px"><div class="container prose-wrap"><article class="prose"><h2>Facts, interpretation and opinion</h2><p>We distinguish factual reporting from our interpretation. When a claim depends on a source, readers should be able to understand where it came from. Commentary and forecasts are presented as judgment, not certainty.</p><h2>Source quality</h2><p>We prefer primary sources where practical: regulators, government data, company filings, official investor materials and original research. Secondary reporting can add context, but it should not replace verification when the primary source is available.</p><h2>Investment coverage</h2><p>Every investment involves trade-offs. When we discuss an opportunity, asset class or market theme, we aim to present meaningful risks alongside potential benefits. Historical performance is not presented as a promise of future returns.</p><h2>Books and financial frameworks</h2><p>We may draw on ideas from respected investors, authors and academics. Their frameworks are treated as ideas to examine, not unquestionable truth. We do not reproduce copyrighted passages beyond what is appropriate for quotation and commentary.</p><h2>Corrections</h2><p>Material factual errors should be corrected promptly and transparently. Editorial updates should not silently rewrite the history of a substantive mistake.</p><h2>Conflicts and disclosures</h2><p>Where relevant, DollarAngle should disclose material relationships, sponsorships or financial interests that could reasonably affect how readers interpret a piece of content.</p></article><aside class="sidebar"><h3>DollarAngle</h3><a href="/about">About</a><a href="/editorial-policy">Editorial Policy</a><a href="/disclaimer">Financial Disclaimer</a><a href="/privacy">Privacy</a><a href="/contact">Contact</a></aside></div></section>
</BaseLayout>
